nts of  funding needs to be put in in these in  these uh  areas and neighborhoods so that the  residents can relive in them again  now uh the problem is uh these  these people have moved back to their  homes because of lack of all  humanitarian aid and services  in at unrest schools but are now lacking  the the basic essentials of life in  their homes  okay thanks very much i'll say reporting  live for us there out of gaza harry  fawcett  joins us live as well from west  jerusalem so harry uh the narrative  both sides claiming a victory here is  being built our on our  that's right yes i mean the israelis to  start with the saying that uh the  civilian restrictions that have been in  place especially the ones very close to  gaza are now being lifted apart from  schools uh they're saying the military  emplacements  will stay there for several days  monitoring the ceasefire  and yes as you say both the israelis and  the  leadership of hamas are claiming victory  hamas is saying that it  exacted concessions out of israel both  in terms of  israeli activities in shekshera and  on the al-aqsa mosque compound known as  the temple mount  to jews the israelis are entirely  denying that saying  it is a lie the israeli line is that  hamas has been put back by years that's  what the defense minister benny gantz  says  in terms of these strikes on the tunnels  on missile production  on senior commanders too in turn hamas  is denying that saying that there are  men proudly striding through the tunnels  as we speak so that is the situation  in terms of the victory claims the there  is a political  criticism of the government of benjamin  netanyahu from the right  one right-wing political leader saying  with the advanced  military and intelligence at his  disposal the only thing that netanyahu  has managed to gain  is a ceasefire without conditions so  criticism coming in on that front too  and the core issues were a long way away  from anyone addressing those  indeed i mean we've seen these this kind  of cyclical  nature of these escalations uh the  smaller ones  maybe every few months these very big  conflicts  maybe every few years but but yes i mean  putting aside the uh the claims of  victory on either side  it does look like things have merely  pretty much gone back to  to where they were before all this  things that have changed i suppose  is for hamas it is a way to say  look we have the people on our side  there are these huge celebrations  not just in gaza but also in the  occupied west bank in  occupied east jerusalem and in some  areas  of palestinian-israeli concentration  within israel  as well and so in its political  competition  with fatah which dominates the  palestinian authority  of president mahmoud abbas you can see  after the  cancellation of the elections by abbas  uh just a few weeks ago that this can be  seen as some kind of uh  of victory on sort of bold political  calculations but those calculations  have to be set aside what the cost is  similarly  with israel saying that it has managed  to set hamas back  we're talking about many people dead  many families grieving and a huge  economic civilian  cost to what's taken place harry for the  moment thank you very much harry fawcett  there for us in west jerusalem  well the u.s president joe biden  welcomed the ceasefire saying his  administration worked  tirelessly behind the scenes gabriel  elizondo has more now from washington  president joe biden had come under  increased pressure over the last week  to do more to try to stop the violence  particularly with  progressives within the democratic party  who were calling on biden  to pressure prime minister benjamin  netanyahu to pursue  more aggressively a ceasefire so  ultimately  when biden did announce this ceasefire  he wanted to show that the u.s was very  much involved in it  and wanted to take some credit we've  held intensive high-level discussions  hour by hour literally egypt the  palestinian authority and other middle  eastern countries  with the name of avoiding the sort of  prolonged conflict we've seen in  previous years when the hostilities have  broken out  i believe the palestinians and israelis  equally deserve to live safely and  securely  and to enjoy equal measures of freedom  prosperity and democracy  my administration will continue our  quiet relentless diplomacy toward that  end  i believe we have a genuine opportunity  to make progress  and i'm committed to working for it the  question now becomes is will the biden  administration  use this opportunity to pursue peace  with israel and palestine in the region  we are getting late word now that  secretary of state anthony blinken will  be heading to the region  in the coming days to meet with both the  israelis and palestinians  and regional players to pursue some sort  of peace agreement  where these meetings will take place and  with exactly whom is still  unknown but it does give an indication  that at least  in some degree bain does want to seize  this opportunity  to pursue a more lasting peace in the  region  well omar badar is a political analyst  and member of the national policy  council at the arab american institute  he says washington has been  complicit in prolonging the conflict  washington holds the primary  responsibility here because it is the  only country that holds significant  leverage over israel and is able to  influence its behavior  but also it's not just a matter of  allowing israel to behave however  once or not but the united states is  specifically  funding the israeli military with  billions of dollars every single year no  other country gets more money  in military funding than israel does and  then the united states runs  interference at the united nations by  using its veto more than 40 times  to veto to block resolutions that would  hold israel accountable at the united  nations  so in effect the united states is  complicit it is responsible for the fact  that nobody can hold israel accountable  for its behavior  and that has to change and we are seeing  that it is beginning to change with that  energy in the democratic party now  with the discourse shifting  substantially within the united states  more celebrities speaking out in defense  of palestinian rights  and members of congress now from from  president biden's own party  demanding that he hold israel  accountable and have a different kind of  politics  things are changing people are becoming  much more aware there is much more  energy within congress itself at this  point  where people are not afraid to speak up  in defense of palestinian rights and not  and are not afraid to challenge the  israel lobby's  influence in washington and are  demanding that the biden administration  make good on its claim  that it's putting human rights first if  the biden is going to claim that human  rights come first in american foreign  policy then you cannot continue  supporting israel's oppression and  crimes against palestinians  well the un secretary general has urged  both sides to respect the truce and  renew dialogue to bring about  reconciliation  and an end to division i welcome the  ceasefire between  gaza and israel after 11 days of deadly  hostilities  i extend my deepest condolences to the  victims of the violence and their loved  ones  i commend egypt and qatar for the  efforts carried out  in close coordination with the u.n to  help restore calm to gaza and israel  i stress that israeli and palestinian  leaders  have a responsibility beyond the  restoration of calm  to start a serious dialogue to address  the root causes of the conflict
 

